if n is odd:
diagonalSum = diagonalSum - matrix[n/2][n/2] //Subtract the center element as it will be added twice for odd sized matrices.

// Calculate boundary sum
for i from 0 to n-1:
for j from 0 to n-1:
if i == 0 or i == n-1 or j == 0 or j == n-1:
boundarySum = boundarySum + matrix[i][j]

//Check if boundary sum is zero
if boundarySum == 0:
return "NO"

// Check if diagonal sum is divisible by boundary sum
if diagonalSum is divisible by boundarySum:
return "YES"
else:
return "NO"
```

## Story
Alice is learning about sorting algorithms in her computer science class. Her instructor posed a challenge: given an array of integers, determine whether it's possible to sort the array into ascending order using only adjacent swaps. An adjacent swap involves exchanging the positions of two adjacent elements in the array. Alice wants to write a program to solve this problem efficiently.

## Problem
You are given an array of integers. Your task is to determine if it is possible to sort the array in ascending order by performing only adjacent swaps. Each swap operation can only be performed between two adjacent elements.

## Input Format
The first line of the input contains a single integer N, representing the number of elements in the array.
The second line contains N space-separated integers, representing the elements of the array.

## Output Format
Print "YES" if it is possible to sort the array using adjacent swaps only. Otherwise, print "NO".

## Constraints
* 1 <= N <= 10^5 (where N is the number of elements in the array)
* 1 <= arr[i] <= 10^9 (where arr[i] is the i-th element in the array)

## Pseudocode
```
function canSortWithAdjacentSwaps(array):
n = length of array
inversions = 0
for i from 0 to n-1:
for j from i+1 to n-1:
if array[i] > array[j]:
inversions = inversions + 1

// Any array can be sorted using bubble sort, which only involves adjacent swaps.
// Therefore, the answer is always YES.
return "YES"
function canSortWithAdjacentSwaps(array):
